# Flow Dependencies And Parallel Work

A Flow is a dependency graph. A node becomes ready when all its predecessor
nodes complete. Result references and explicit `after:` fields create edges.
Source order does not create edges.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowDependencies.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_dependencies_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Build A Diamond

The `implicit` node reads `source`, so that result reference creates an edge.
The `explicit` node reads only Flow input, but `after: "source"` creates the
same control edge. `left` and `right` are independent roots.

```elixir
defmodule FlowDependencies.Diamond do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_dependencies_diamond",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    step "implicit",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{value: select(result("source"), :value)}

    step "explicit",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)},
      after: "source"

    step "left",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    step "right",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    step "joined",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{
        implicit: select(result("implicit"), :value),
        explicit: select(result("explicit"), :value),
        left: select(result("left"), :value),
        right: select(result("right"), :value)
      }
  end
end
```

Inspect the direct predecessors:

```elixir
flow = FlowDependencies.Diamond.flow()
{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(flow)

["source"] = dependencies["implicit"]
["source"] = dependencies["explicit"]
["explicit", "implicit", "left", "right"] = dependencies["joined"]
[] = dependencies["left"]
[] = dependencies["right"]
```

## Ready Waves Are Canonical

Start a paused execution to inspect the graph. `ready/1` returns ready node
names in canonical order. `wave/1` executes the current ready set. Nodes that
become ready wait for the next wave.

```elixir
{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.start(FlowDependencies.Diamond, %{value: 2}, %{})

["left", "right", "source"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, first_wave, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)
true = Enum.map(first_wave, & &1.node) == ["left", "right", "source"]

["explicit", "implicit"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, _second_wave, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)
["joined"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.continue(execution)
{:ok, %{implicit: 2, explicit: 2, left: 2, right: 2}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

## Parallel Structure Is Implicit

There is no `parallel` block in the DSL. The graph already shows which nodes
are independent. Set `async: true` and `max_concurrency` at execution time:

```elixir
{:ok, %{implicit: 2, explicit: 2, left: 2, right: 2}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowDependencies.Diamond,
    %{value: 2},
    %{},
    async: true,
    max_concurrency: 3
  )
```

The execution option applies to ready waves. It does not change dependencies.

## Choose `after:` Only For Control Order

Prefer a result reference when a node needs predecessor data. Use `after:`
when the dependency exists only for control order, such as an audit that must
run after a write but does not need the write output. `after:` accepts one node
name or a list of names.

